# `gate-bite-harness` writes a mutated COPY of a gate beside the original while
# it measures whether that gate's self-test bites, and removes it in a `finally`.
# A killed run leaves one behind -- which has happened -- so it must be
# impossible to commit rather than merely unlikely.
scripts/.bite-*.py


# ── Agent workflow: what is SHARED vs what is YOURS ─────────────────────────
# The agent workflow IS committed on purpose (see docs/standards/agent-workflow.md):
# when several people and several agents work the same repo, a shared, versioned
# process is what makes a wrong turn reviewable instead of invisible. Committed:
# AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, .claude/, .cursor/, .codex/, .agents/, .github/skills/,
# .ai-factory/, .githooks/, scripts/*-gate.py.
#
# Personal layer — never committed. Put your own overrides here and they stay
# yours without forking anyone else's process:
.ai-factory/skill-context.local/
.claude/settings.local.json
.codex/config.local.toml
.vscode/settings.json
AGENTS.local.md

# scratch backups made while biting (see AGENTS.md: BITE)
*.bak
.vlive*/
.vcode*/
.vmetric*/

# Gate verdict caches: LOCAL accelerators keyed on a tree digest. Never committed — CI runs
# both gates with --force, and a verdict from one machine certifies nothing on another.
contracts/*-verdict.json
scripts/toolloop/fe_runner_auth.json

# Tool-loop fixture provenance manifests — live state about this machine's runs, written by
# scripts/toolloop/provision.py so `--sweep` can remove an un-torn-down fixture BY ID.
scripts/toolloop/.fixtures/
